# Overview
#
# `may not` reads two ways, as a prohibition (`the name may not be a
# dot`) or as a possibility (`the cache may not be warm`), and the
# kubernetes docs use both. The reader has to pick the reading from the
# words around the phrase. The fix writes `must not` for a prohibition
# and `might not` for a possibility. The rule sits at Recommended,
# because the fix depends on the reading that the writer means.
#
# Expression
#
# `main` matches the two words. `may or may not` counts too, because it
# ends in the same words and hedges the claim as well.
#
# Related Rules
#
# ProseMayPossibility reports `may` in other phrases and skips a
# following `not`, so only this rule reports `may not`.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 121 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 20 of 20 sampled
# hits took one of the two fixes. Of the 20, 15 stated a possibility and
# 5 stated a prohibition (`you may not change or remove an ephemeral
# container`).
expr main may not
let message "`may not`. Write `must not` for a prohibition, or `might not` for a possibility."
let description "Flags `may not`, which reads as a prohibition or as a possibility."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The name must not be a dot."
allows "The cache might not be warm."
